Acute Myeloid Leukaemia (AML)
Haematology & Oncology
Gingival hypertrophy/bleeding, Recurrent severe infections, Sternal tenderness, Auer rods on blood smear

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-002
Reason for Visit: Spontaneous and severe mucosal bleeding with profound systemic malaise requiring urgent assessment to exclude acute coagulopathy and prevent critical overnight deterioration.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a rapid, insidious onset of unprovoked gingival bleeding that would not stop, coupled with recurrent fevers. Urgent physical evaluation was required to assess haemodynamic stability and rule out life-threatening marrow failure, which could not safely wait until the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My gums started bleeding heavily this afternoon for no reason, and I've felt completely drained and feverish for the last few days."
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a rapid insidious onset of profound fatigue and systemic malaise developing over the past week. Three days ago, they developed a high fever and a sore throat. This afternoon, they experienced spontaneous, severe gingival bleeding without brushing or trauma, which has been continuous. They also note a dull, aching pain in their chest bone.
Functional Status: Independent but currently resting in a chair due to extreme fatigue.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; high risk of unobserved syncope or severe haemorrhage.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 112 | Temp: 38.2 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Pale, visibly exhausted adult. Continuous mild oozing from the gums.
Physical Exam:
HEENT: Pronounced gingival hypertrophy with spontaneous oozing of blood. Pallor of the conjunctivae.
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm. No murmurs.
Respiratory: Clear bases bilaterally.
Skin: Scattered petechiae and widespread purpura on the lower extremities. Violaceous raised skin lesions (leukaemia cutis) noted on the left forearm.
Musculoskeletal: Exquisite sternal tenderness elicited on light palpation.
Neurological: Intact, no gross deficits. Minimal lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Blood-soaked tissues in the bin; clear pathways to the exit.
Medication Reconciliation: No current anticoagulants or NSAIDs detected.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Myeloid Leukaemia (AML) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Scurvy (Vitamin C Deficiency) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: presence of sternal tenderness, systemic fever, and lack of characteristic dietary history.

Warfarin/DOAC Toxicity - Ruled out/unlikely due to: verified absence of anticoagulant medication use and presence of high fever and gingival hypertrophy.

Aplastic Anaemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: presence of gingival hypertrophy, leukaemia cutis, and severe sternal tenderness.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation of spontaneous mucosal bleeding, petechiae, fever, and sternal tenderness strongly indicates acute bone marrow failure secondary to a haematological malignancy, specifically raising suspicion for AML. The continuous gingival oozing combined with tachycardia and fever dictates that delaying assessment and intervention until normal clinic hours poses an imminent risk of haemodynamic collapse, severe sepsis, or fatal intracranial haemorrhage. This justifies an urgent after-hours home visit to establish clinical stability and facilitate immediate hospital admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department for urgent full blood count, coagulation studies, and peripheral smear (assessing for >20% myeloblasts and Auer rods). Intravenous access and blood products will likely be required inpatient.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Apply firm, continuous pressure to bleeding sites using clean gauze. By maintaining constant compression, you assist the body's mechanical clotting process and reduce blood loss.

Avoid any sudden movements or Valsalva maneuvers (straining). By keeping your blood pressure stable, you reduce the risk of dislodging fragile clots or inducing internal bleeding.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate ED transfer overrides routine GP follow-up.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate Emergency Department transfer is required. Call 000 for ambulance transport if you feel dizzy, confused, or if the bleeding suddenly worsens.

